## 2. EC2 Instance Management

### 2.1 — Connect to Your EC2 Instance

```bash
# From your Windows machine (PowerShell)
ssh -i "your-key.pem" ec2-user@<YOUR_ELASTIC_IP>

# If using Ubuntu AMI
ssh -i "your-key.pem" ubuntu@<YOUR_ELASTIC_IP>
```

### 2.2 — Essential EC2 Commands (on the server)

```bash
# --- System health ---
top                      # CPU/Memory live view
df -h                    # Disk usage (IMPORTANT: Docker images fill this fast!)
free -h                  # RAM usage

# --- Update the OS (run monthly) ---
sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y    # Ubuntu
sudo yum update -y                        # Amazon Linux

# --- Check uptime & reboot history ---
uptime
last reboot
```

### 2.3 — Elastic IP (CRITICAL)

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Always assign an Elastic IP** to your EC2 instance. Without it, every time the instance restarts, your public IP changes — breaking your domain/DNS.

```
AWS Console → EC2 → Elastic IPs → Allocate → Associate with Instance
```

Cost: **Free** as long as the Elastic IP is associated with a running instance.

### 2.4 — Instance Auto-Start on Reboot

Make Docker Compose start automatically when the EC2 instance reboots:

```bash
# On your EC2 server — create a systemd service
sudo nano /etc/systemd/system/ai-pulse.service
```

Paste this content:

```ini
[Unit]
Description=AI-Pulse Docker Compose Application
Requires=docker.service
After=docker.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
RemainAfterExit=yes
WorkingDirectory=/home/ubuntu/AI-PULSE-PREDICTIVE-INVENTORY-MANAGEMENT-SYSTEM-
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.prod.yml up -d
ExecStop=/usr/local/bin/docker compose down
TimeoutStartSec=0

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
```

```bash
# Enable the service
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl enable ai-pulse.service

# Test it
sudo systemctl start ai-pulse.service
sudo systemctl status ai-pulse.service
```

