## 4. IAM — Identity & Access Management

### 4.1 — The Golden Rule

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Never use your AWS root account for daily tasks.** Create an IAM user with only the permissions it needs.

### 4.2 — Recommended IAM Setup for AI-Pulse

**For your personal admin access (yourself):**
```
IAM → Users → Create User
  → Attach policy: "AdministratorAccess" (for your personal user)
  → Enable MFA (Multi-Factor Authentication) — MANDATORY
```

**For GitHub Actions CI/CD (programmatic access only):**
```
IAM → Users → Create User: "ai-pulse-deploy-bot"
  → Attach a custom policy (minimum permissions):
```

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": [
        "ec2:DescribeInstances",
        "ssm:SendCommand",
        "ssm:GetCommandInvocation"
      ],
      "Resource": "*"
    }
  ]
}
```

> [!TIP]
> Use **IAM Roles** instead of access keys wherever possible. Assign an EC2 IAM Role to your instance so it can access S3/CloudWatch without storing keys in `.env` files.

### 4.3 — Store Secrets Safely (Not in .env files on EC2!)

Instead of hardcoding secrets in `backend/.env` on the server, use **AWS Secrets Manager**:

```bash
# Store a secret
aws secretsmanager create-secret \
  --name "ai-pulse/prod/db-password" \
  --secret-string "YourDBPassword123!"

# Retrieve it in your startup script
DB_PASSWORD=$(aws secretsmanager get-secret-value \
  --secret-id "ai-pulse/prod/db-password" \
  --query SecretString --output text)
```

