## 6. Database Management

### 6.1 — MySQL Container vs AWS RDS

| Feature | MySQL in Docker (Current) | AWS RDS MySQL |
|---------|--------------------------|---------------|
| **Cost** | Free (included in EC2) | ~$15-30/month (db.t3.micro) |
| **Backups** | Manual | Automated daily snapshots |
| **Failover** | None | Multi-AZ option |
| **Scaling** | Manual | Easy vertical scaling |
| **Maintenance** | You manage | AWS manages |
| **Best for** | Dev / Demo / Portfolio | Production with real users |

> [!TIP]
> For a **portfolio/demo project**, MySQL in Docker is perfectly fine. If you're pitching this to an employer as a production system, mention you'd migrate to **RDS** for high availability and automated backups.

### 6.2 — Database Backup (Manual — Run Weekly)

```bash
# On EC2 — Backup MySQL from running container
docker exec ai_pulse_db mysqldump \
  -u root -p"${DB_PASSWORD}" \
  ai_pulse_db > backup_$(date +%Y%m%d_%H%M%S).sql

# Upload backup to S3
aws s3 cp backup_*.sql s3://your-ai-pulse-backups/mysql/
```

### 6.3 — Automated Backup Script

```bash
# Create backup script
sudo nano /home/ubuntu/backup-db.sh
```

```bash
#!/bin/bash
TIMESTAMP=$(date +%Y%m%d_%H%M%S)
BACKUP_DIR="/home/ubuntu/backups"
mkdir -p $BACKUP_DIR

# Dump database
docker exec ai_pulse_db mysqldump \
  -u root -p"${DB_PASSWORD}" ai_pulse_db \
  > "$BACKUP_DIR/ai_pulse_$TIMESTAMP.sql"

# Upload to S3 (requires IAM role with S3 write permission)
aws s3 cp "$BACKUP_DIR/ai_pulse_$TIMESTAMP.sql" \
  s3://your-ai-pulse-backups/mysql/

# Delete local backups older than 7 days
find $BACKUP_DIR -name "*.sql" -mtime +7 -delete

echo "Backup completed: ai_pulse_$TIMESTAMP.sql"
```

```bash
# Schedule with cron — run every Sunday at 2 AM
chmod +x /home/ubuntu/backup-db.sh
crontab -e
# Add this line:
0 2 * * 0 /home/ubuntu/backup-db.sh >> /home/ubuntu/backup.log 2>&1
```

