## 11. Scaling Strategy

### 11.1 — Current (Single EC2 — Portfolio Scale)

Good for: Portfolio demos, up to ~100 concurrent users.

### 11.2 — Phase 2 (If you get real users)

```
                    ┌─────────────────┐
Internet ──────────►│  Load Balancer  │  (AWS ALB)
                    │  (port 80/443)  │
                    └────────┬────────┘
                             │
                    ┌────────▼────────┐
                    │   EC2 Instance  │  (Auto Scaling Group)
                    │   Frontend +    │
                    │   Backend       │
                    └────────┬────────┘
                             │
                    ┌────────▼────────┐
                    │   RDS MySQL     │  (Managed, Multi-AZ)
                    │   (Separate)    │
                    └─────────────────┘
```

### 11.3 — Interview-Ready Answer on Scaling

> *"Currently the system runs as Docker containers on a single EC2 instance, which is appropriate for the current load. To scale, I'd introduce an Application Load Balancer with an Auto Scaling Group for the stateless frontend/backend services, migrate the MySQL database from Docker to RDS for managed backups and Multi-AZ failover, and add ElastiCache (Redis) for session caching. The Docker Compose architecture makes this migration straightforward — the same images can be deployed to ECS or EKS with minimal changes."*

