## 12. Incident Response Runbook

### 🔴 Site is Down — First 5 Minutes

```bash
# Step 1: Check if EC2 is running
AWS Console → EC2 → Instances → check State = "running"

# Step 2: SSH into server
ssh -i "your-key.pem" ubuntu@<ELASTIC_IP>

# Step 3: Check containers
docker compose ps
# Are all 3 services "Up (healthy)"?

# Step 4: Check logs for errors
docker compose logs --tail=50 backend
docker compose logs --tail=50 frontend
docker compose logs --tail=50 db

# Step 5: Restart everything
docker compose down
docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.prod.yml up -d

# Step 6: Test health endpoint
curl -f http://localhost/api/v1/health
curl -f https://localhost/ --insecure
```

### 🔴 Database Issues

```bash
# Check if DB container is healthy
docker inspect ai_pulse_db | grep Health -A 5

# Connect to MySQL inside container
docker exec -it ai_pulse_db mysql -u root -p

# Check DB size
SELECT table_schema, ROUND(SUM(data_length + index_length) / 1024 / 1024, 2) AS "Size (MB)"
FROM information_schema.tables
GROUP BY table_schema;
```

### 🔴 Disk Full

```bash
# Check disk
df -h

# Emergency cleanup (safe)
docker system prune -f

# Check large files
du -sh /var/lib/docker/volumes/*
du -sh /home/ubuntu/*

# Delete old logs
sudo journalctl --vacuum-time=7d
```

### 🟡 High Memory / CPU

```bash
# Check which container is consuming resources
docker stats

# Restart the offending service
docker compose restart backend

# Check for memory leaks in logs
docker compose logs backend | grep -i "heap\|memory\|OOM"
```

